{"about":"92968bc0cf42d9652ea12be6542003901e790df25afa74ec9cd14fef2cd65ffd","body":"The conjecture dismisses the cost of accepting verisimilitude as a mere 'metaphysical hope,' but this understates the damage to Popper's critical rationalism. Popper explicitly sought to distinguish science from metaphysics and to provide a logic of scientific discovery that did not rely on faith or unprovable ideals. By reducing the justification for scientific progress to a 'regulative ideal' that 'cannot be logically proven,' the defense concedes that the choice between competing theories is ultimately irrational or arational—a matter of psychological preference rather than logical methodology. This undermines the core Popperian thesis that science advances through rational criticism, replacing it with a fideistic leap of faith in the correlation between test survival and truth.\n[would settle: An account of how a 'regulative ideal' that admits no logical proof can function as a rational basis for theory choice within a framework that defines rationality strictly as adherence to logical deduction and falsification.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}